{%- comment -%}
  Review prompt — the storefront popup, for a customer who has received
  something and not been asked about it.
  Usage: {% render 'review-prompt' %} from overlays, so it is on every template.

  Why it lives here and not on the order status page. Shopify does not allow a
  modal to open on its own after checkout — Thank you, Order status and the
  customer account pages all forbid it, and the platform is explicit that a
  modal opens on buyer interaction or not at all. Our own storefront has no
  such rule, so the ask that has to appear by itself has to appear here.

  Three gates, and all of them have to pass before any markup is written.

  Signed in. customer.orders is only readable for a logged-in customer, which
  is also the only way to know what they bought. Guests get nothing, and most
  cash-on-delivery buyers are guests — this reaches the minority who have an
  account, which is why the Judge.me request email is still the mechanism that
  does the real work.

  Fulfilled. Asking about a bottle that has not arrived is the same mistake as
  asking at checkout. The loop skips every order that is not fulfilled rather
  than taking the most recent one and hoping.

  A real product on the line. A line item whose product has since been deleted
  has no URL to send anyone to, so it is skipped instead of rendering a card
  that goes nowhere.

  One order, one product, one ask. The most recent fulfilled order that has
  anything promptable in it; the first line with a product behind it. A popup
  offering four things to review is a menu, and a menu gets dismissed.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  # A flag rather than testing an unset variable: Liquid has no nil literal,
  # and "assign x = blank" leans on behaviour that is easy to misread later.
  assign rp_found = false

  if customer and customer.orders.size > 0
    for o in customer.orders
      unless o.fulfillment_status == 'fulfilled'
        continue
      endunless
      for line in o.line_items
        if line.product == blank
          continue
        endif
        assign rp_order = o
        assign rp_item = line
        assign rp_found = true
        break
      endfor
      if rp_found
        break
      endif
    endfor
  endif
-%}

{%- if rp_found -%}
  {%- liquid
    assign rp_product = rp_item.product
    # Keyed on both, so a second order of the same scent may be asked about
    # again while a dismissal of this one sticks.
    assign rp_key = 'tfs_rp_' | append: rp_order.id | append: '_' | append: rp_product.id
  -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    hidden until app.js decides, so it cannot flash on first paint. The
    destination is a real href on every star: with no JavaScript the card
    never shows, but if it ever does, every control still goes somewhere.

    The rating rides along as a query parameter the same way the order-page
    card sends it. Judge.me decides whether to honour it on the product form;
    if it does not, the shopper picks again on a form they were going to be
    shown anyway, so the click is never wasted.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="rev-prompt" id="revPrompt" data-key="{{ rp_key }}" data-path="{{ rp_product.url }}" hidden>
    <button type="button" class="rev-prompt-x" data-rp-dismiss aria-label="Not now">
      <span class="mi" aria-hidden="true">close</span>
    </button>

    <div class="rev-prompt-body">
      {%- if rp_item.image -%}
        <img class="rev-prompt-img" src="{{ rp_item.image | image_url: width: 140 }}"
             alt="" width="64" height="64" loading="lazy">
      {%- endif -%}

      <div class="rev-prompt-copy">
        <p class="rev-prompt-title">How was it?</p>
        <p class="rev-prompt-name">{{ rp_product.title | truncate: 58 }}</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      Each star is its own link, labelled with what it does: a screen reader
      meets five distinct controls rather than five identical icons, and the
      whole thing works without JavaScript driving it.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="rev-prompt-stars" role="group" aria-label="Rate {{ rp_product.title | escape }}">
      {%- for i in (1..5) -%}
        <a class="rev-prompt-star" href="{{ rp_product.url }}?jdgm_rating={{ i }}#judgeme_product_reviews"
           data-rp-go aria-label="Rate {{ i }} out of 5 and write a review">
          <span class="mi mi-f" aria-hidden="true">star</span>
        </a>
      {%- endfor -%}
    </div>

    <p class="rev-prompt-note">Ordered {{ rp_order.created_at | date: '%-d %b' }} &middot; takes a few seconds</p>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
